      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "A family tree of scripts"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Egyptian hieroglyphs → Proto-Sinaitic → Phoenician. From Phoenician: Greek (which added vowels), then Latin, Cyrillic and Gothic; Aramaic, then Hebrew, Arabic and probably Brāhmī."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Brāhmī → Devanagari, Tamil, Tibetan, Thai, and the Tocharian script of the Tarim Basin."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Cyrillic was created in the 9th–10th centuries, after Glagolitic, for Old Church Slavonic; it is named after Saint Cyril."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Decipherments"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Egyptian hieroglyphs: Champollion, 1822, with the Rosetta Stone."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Linear B: Michael Ventris, 1952; it turned out to be Mycenaean Greek."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Hittite cuneiform: Bedřich Hrozný, 1915, showing it was Indo-European."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Linear A and the Indus script are still undeciphered."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Spelling and sound"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Shallow orthographies match sound closely (Italian, Finnish); deep ones keep history (English, French). French eaux [o]: five letters, one sound."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "The IPA exists because no ordinary alphabet is consistent."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Direction"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "text": "Left to right (Latin, Cyrillic), right to left (Arabic, Hebrew), top to bottom (traditional Chinese, Japanese), and boustrophedon, alternating like an ox ploughing (early Greek)."
      }
